{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789223268,"nonce":"lQJjQZVAoX7VqEMJ/v/IlwdU8Os=","title":"A block expression used as a statement dropped the statements around it","message":"`TypedCfgBuilder::split_at_control_flow` handled\n`TypedStatement::Expression(TypedExpression::Block(..))` by recursing\nwith the current block id and no flush. The statements accumulated\nbefore the block were never emitted (the recursion started a new\n`current_statements` under the same id), and the statements after it\nwere appended to the block the recursion had already closed, so they\nwere emitted twice or not at all depending on the exit shape.\n\nReproducer (Python frontend, which emits a Block for `print(a, b)`):\n\n    x = 1\n    print(x, 2)\n    print(\"after\")\n\nLowered through typed_cfg, `x = 1` vanished and `print(\"after\")`\nlanded on the recursion's exit id. The Python `print(a, b)` desugar is\nthe first producer of a bare Block statement; ZynML never emits one,\nwhich is why this arm had no coverage.\n\nFix: treat it exactly like `TypedStatement::Block`: flush preceding\nstatements into a block that jumps to a fresh entry, recurse into that\nentry, retarget the recursion's dangling exit to a fresh after-block,\nand continue accumulating there.","files":null}]}